-- DuckDB CLI startup file. Sibling of _psqlrc; the same habits should hold in
-- both shells.
--
-- Read on every `duckdb` start, including through `csvsql`, so anything added
-- here applies to both. csvsql layers its own settings on top afterward.

-- No `.timer on` here, even though _psqlrc has `\timing on`. DuckDB writes the
-- "Run Time" line to STDOUT, not stderr, so it lands in the middle of the data
-- whenever output is redirected: `duckdb -csv -c "..." > out.csv` ends up with
-- a trailing junk row. csvsql turns the timer on only when stdout is a
-- terminal; type `.timer on` by hand in a plain duckdb session.

-- No pager, matching `\pset pager off` in _psqlrc. DuckDB's default pipes any
-- result over 50 rows or wider than the terminal into a pager, which is
-- actively wrong inside the nvim SQL workspace: the terminal pane is short, so
-- almost every result opens a pager, and the next thing typed goes to the pager
-- instead of to DuckDB. Results scroll in the pane's own scrollback instead.
.pager off

-- No .maxwidth here. DuckDB already renders at the terminal width, and it
-- clamps the setting to a floor of 80 columns, so a table cannot be made to fit
-- a pane narrower than that: rows just wrap. In a narrow pane either take the
-- width back (<leader>n closes the file browser in the nvim SQL workspace) or
-- switch to `.mode line`, which is DuckDB's answer to `\pset expanded`.

-- 40 rows is a small preview and cutting it off mid-thought is worse than
-- scrolling. Still bounded, so `select *` on a million rows cannot flood the
-- terminal.
.maxrows 100

-- Point at the offending token when a query fails, but leave live input
-- highlighting off: it recolors the line as you type and fights with pasted
-- multi-line SQL.
.highlight off
.highlight_errors on
